\subsection*{Spectral Decomposition}\label{SectionSpectralDecomposition}
We now turn to the spectral decomposition of the operator $T$, which is a key tool in the study of its properties. The spectral decomposition theorem states that if $T$ is a bounded linear operator on a Hilbert space $H$, then there exists a projection-valued measure $\mu$ on the spectrum $\sigma(T)$ of $T$ such that for every $x \in H$, the action of $T$ on $x$ can be expressed as
\begin{equation*}
T x = \int_{\sigma(T)} \lambda \, d\mu(\lambda) \cdot x.
\end{equation*}
Here, $\lambda$ represents the eigenvalues of $T$, and the measure $\mu$ is defined on the Borel subsets of $\sigma(T)$. The integral is understood in the strong operator topology. This decomposition provides a powerful framework for analyzing the operator $T$, as it allows us to break down the action of $T$ into a sum of projections, each corresponding to an eigenvalue of $T$. This section will explore the conditions under which this decomposition holds and discuss some of its implications.

