This implies that the Jacobi function $h(\rho)$ is strictly positive for all $\rho$ in the interval under consideration. Therefore, the equation
\begin{equation}
\label{h_condition}
h(\rho) = 0
\end{equation}
has no solutions other than $\rho=0$. This condition is crucial for the existence of an extremum of the functional defined by the Lagrangian $L(\Theta, \dot{\Theta})$ with the boundary conditions $\Theta(0)=\Theta(L)$ and $\dot{\Theta}(0)=\dot{\Theta}(L)$. In other words, the extremum exists if and only if the Jacobi function $h(\rho)$ does not vanish anywhere in the interval $[0, L]$. This property is often referred to as the **Jacobi criterion** for the existence of an extremum in the calculus of variations.

