To solve the system of equations numerically, we employ the shooting method. We define the following functions:
\begin{align*}
    f_1(\rho) &= \Theta(\rho) - \text{solution of Eq.~(\ref{Lagrange_dims})}, \\
    f_2(\rho) &= h(\rho) - \text{solution of Eq.~(\ref{Jacobi_dims})}.
\end{align*}
We seek a $\rho_0$ such that $f_1(\rho_0)=0$ and $f_2(\rho_0)=0$. This defines a shooting problem. We start with an initial guess $\rho_0$ and iteratively adjust it until both conditions are satisfied within a chosen tolerance. The procedure is repeated for different values of $d^2/AK$ to explore the parameter space. The results are shown in Figs.~\ref{fig:theta_h} and \ref{fig:phase_diagram}.
