The $\mathbb{Z}_2$ invariant is a powerful tool for the classification of topological insulators and superconductors. It provides a way to distinguish between different topological phases by assigning a quantum number to each phase, which is invariant under continuous deformations of the system. This invariant is particularly useful in the context of one-dimensional systems, where it can be used to predict the presence of edge states and their properties.

In the context of zigzag graphene nanoribbons (ZGNR), the $\mathbb{Z}_2$ invariant has been extensively studied due to their potential applications in electronic and spintronic devices. For ZGNR-C, where the ribbons have cove-shaped edges, the $\mathbb{Z}_2$ invariant can be used to determine the nature of the edge states and their degeneracy.

The $\mathbb{Z}_2$ invariant for a one-dimensional system can be defined as follows:
\[
Z_2 = \begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if the system is trivial (no edge states)} \\
    1 & \text{if the system supports non-trivial edge states}
\end{cases}
\]

