\section*{Friedmann--Lema\^{\i}tre Equations}

The Friedmann--Lema\^{\i}tre equations describe the dynamics of the universe on large scales. They are derived from Einstein's field equations of general relativity and provide a set of ordinary differential equations for the scale factor $R(t)$ of the universe. The key quantities are the density $\rho$, the curvature $K$, and the cosmological constant $\Lambda$. The Friedmann equations can be written in the form:

\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{\dot{R}}{R}\right)^2 &= \frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho + \frac{\Lambda}{3} - \frac{K}{R^2} \\
&\quad + \frac{2}{3} \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{\delta m_i}{R^3}, \\
\dot{R} &= \sqrt{\frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho + \frac{\Lambda}{3} - \frac{K}{R^2} + \frac{2}{3} \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{\delta m_i}{R^3}}

