\subsection{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The results presented in the previous sections provide a comprehensive overview of our approach to learning and transferring embedding spaces. In this section, we discuss the implications of our findings and compare them to existing methods in the literature.

Firstly, the ability to learn shared embedding spaces across different cues demonstrates the potential for our method to generalize well to new, unseen cues. This generalization is crucial in real-world applications where the set of available cues can vary significantly. By leveraging a shared representation, our method can potentially achieve better performance than cue-specific approaches, which may struggle to capture the underlying structure of the data when faced with novel cues.

Secondly, the success of transferring learned embedding spaces to new tasks highlights the robustness and transferability of our approach. This capability is particularly valuable in scenarios where resources are limited, and it is impractical to re-train models from scratch. By transferring learned embeddings, we can leverage the knowledge captured in one domain to solve related problems in another, thus reducing the computational burden and potentially improving performance.

