In the following we propose a set of Laplace transform sumrules (LSR) which can help to clarify such discrepancies. We shall be concerned here with the $V$-$A$ two-point correlator:
\begin{eqnarray}
&\Pi_{\mu\nu}^{LR}(q)\equiv i\int d^4 x~ e^{iqx}\langle 0|{\cal T} J_\mu^L(x)\left(J_\nu^R(0)\right)^{\dagger}|0\rangle\nonumber\\&\stackrel{m_q\rightarrow 0}{=}-(g_{\mu\nu}q^2-q_\mu q_\nu)\Pi^{LR}(q^2)~,
\end{eqnarray}
built from the left-- and right--handed components of the local weak current:
\begin{equation}
J_\mu^{L}=\bar u\gamma_\mu(1-\gamma_5)d,~~~~~~~J_\mu^{R}=\bar
u\gamma^\mu(1+\gamma_5)d~.\end{equation}Following SVZ \cite{SVZ}, the correlator can be approximated by:\begin{equation}\Pi^{LR}(Q^2)\simeq\sum_{d\geq 2}{{\cal O}_{2d}\over (Q^2)^d}\end{equation}where ${\cal O}_{2d}\equiv C_{2d}\langle{\cal O}_{2d}\rangle$ is the short hand-notation of the the QCD non-perturbativecondensates $\langle{\cal O}_{2d}\rangle$ of dimension$D\equiv 2d$ and its associated perturbative Wilson coefficient $C_{2d}$; $q^2\equiv -(Q^2 >0)$ is the momentum transfer. In the chiral limit $m_{u,d}=0$, there is no $D=2$ term as unflavored contribution of the renormalon-type\cite{ZAKA,ZAKA1,ZAKA2} vanishes. The spectral function $(v-a)$:\begin{equation}\frac{1}{\pi}{\rm Im}\Pi^{LR}\equiv\frac{1}{2\pi^2}\left( v-a\right)~.\end{equation}has been measured byALEPH and OPAL \cite{ALEPH,OPAL} using$\tau$-decay data. Within a such normalization, the original ``sacrosante" first and secondWeinberg sum rules \cite{WEINBERG,DMO} read, in the chiral limit $m_{u,d}=0$:\begin{eqnarray}{\cal S}_{0}&\equiv&\int_0^{\infty} dt ~\frac{1}{\pi}{\rm Im}\Pi_{LR} -2f^2_\pi=0~,\nonumber\\{\cal S}_{1}&\equiv&\int_0^{\infty} dt ~t~\frac{1}{\pi}{\rm Im}\Pi_{LR} =
0~,\end{eqnarray}where $f_\pi=(92.4\pm 0.26)$ MeV is the experimental pion decay
constant.

